WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 228: Poland-Lithuania: The Winged Hussars, King Jan Sobieski, and the Relief of Vienna (1683)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.080
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:14.720
[Rebecca] Today, we ride onto the thunderous battlefields of 17th century Europe to witness one of the most thrilling cavalry charges in human history.

3
00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:26.640
[Rebecca] King Jans Obiski and the legendary winged hussars of Poland Lithuania rescuing Vienna from Ottoman conquest in 1683.

4
00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:37.920
[George] In the late 17th century, the Polish Lithuanian Commonwealth was one of the largest and most culturally vibrant realms in Europe, stretching from the Baltic Sea almost to the Black Sea.

5
00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:51.600
[George] It was a unique noble republic, governed by elected kings and an aristocratic parliament, where Catholic, Orthodox, Protestant, Jewish, and Muslim Tatar populations coexisted within a sprawling Commonwealth.

6
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.800
[George] But in the spring of 1683, an existential storm gathered in the east.

7
00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:15.360
[George] Grand Vizier Kara Mustafa Pasha led a colossal Ottoman army of over 150,000 soldiers, Janissaries and Spahis marching into Central Europe, determined to capture Vienna, the golden apple of the Habsburg Empire, and push the borders of Islam into the heart of the continent.

8
00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:29.520
[George] Emperor Leopold fled Vienna in panic, while the heroic city garrison under Count Ernst Rudiger von Starhemberg braced for a brutal siege behind battered ramparts, desperately pleading for Christian Europe to come to their salvation.

9
00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:42.480
[George] The Polish Lithuanian Commonwealth stretched from the pine forests of the Baltic coast to the sun baked steppes of the Dnieper, encompassing a diverse commonwealth where over 30 languages were spoken across twelve provinces.

10
00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:55.520
[George] In the Commonwealth, the Schlacht maintained an unwritten code of chivalric honor, viewing the defense of the eastern frontiers of Christian Europe as their sacred providential destiny against Tatar and Ottoman incursions.

11
00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:59.760
[George] Pope Innocent Exai celebrated a solemn high mass of Thanksgiving in St.

12
00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:09.560
[George] Peter's Basilica, conferring upon Jan Sobiski the title of defender of the faith, while church bells rang across Christian Europe from London to Madrid.

13
00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:20.440
[George] The one man in Europe possessing the military stature, charisma, and courage to challenge the Ottoman host was the newly elected King of Poland, Jan Iai Sobiski.

14
00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:36.600
[George] Born into an ancient Polish noble warrior family, Sobiski was a seasoned military genius, fluent in multiple languages, deeply cultured, and a veteran of dozens of ferocious campaigns against Turks, Tatars, and Cossacks across the wild steppes.

15
00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:46.040
[George] In the spring of 1683, Sobiski concluded a mutual defensive alliance with Habsburg Austria, brokered by Pope Innocent Exai.

16
00:02:46.040 --> 00:03:01.320
[George] When desperate couriers arrived from Vienna warning that the imperial capital had only days before Ottoman sappers breached the city walls with gunpowder mines, Sobiski did not hesitate, leaving Warsaw at the head of 25,000 elite Polish troops.

17
00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:15.640
[George] Sobiski marched his army over 300 miles through mountain passes in just twelve days, joining Austrian and German imperial forces to form a grand Christian relief coalition of 70,000 men under his unified supreme command.

18
00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:27.080
[George] Sobiski was a veteran of the bloody battle of Chazim, where his tactical genius had shattered an Ottoman army and earned him the awe of European Christendom, who hailed him as the Northern Lion.

19
00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:41.720
[George] Pope Innocent Exai had pledged substantial papal subsidies to finance Sobiska's army, sending his energetic papal legate Marco De Viano to spiritually unify the squabbling Austrian, German, and Polish military commanders.

20
00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:54.640
[George] The winged hussars were equipped with iron sabers known as shabblas, curved specifically for devastating cavalry slashes from the saddle, their blades engraved with gold crosses and invocations of divine protection.

21
00:03:54.640 --> 00:04:03.600
[George] The terrifying vanguard of Sobiska's army was the winged hussars, the most feared, visually striking heavy cavalry in military history.

22
00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:10.160
[George] Recruited exclusively from the wealthy Polish nobility, each hussar was equipped with an iron breastplate.

23
00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:22.160
[George] A velvet cape lined with leopard or wolf pelt, a Turkish curved saber, pistols, and a devastating hollowwood lance measuring up to twenty feet in length, longer than any infantry pike.

24
00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:32.800
[George] Most extraordinary were the iconic wooden wings attached to their saddles or armor, arching high above their heads and adorned with hundreds of eagle, swan or vulture feathers.

25
00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:36.640
[George] In battle, as thousands of hussars charged at full gallop.

26
00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:51.360
[George] The rush of wind through the feathers produced an eerie, thundering whistle, while the massive fluttering wings made the horsemen appear superhumanly tall, terrifying opposing infantry and frightening enemy horses, so they refused to stand their ground.

27
00:04:51.360 --> 00:05:01.760
[George] For over a century, the winged hussars had remained practically undefeated across Eastern Europe, an elite strike force designed for pure, unstoppable shock warfare.

28
00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:15.680
[George] Each hussar spent an entire fortune on his warhorse, breeding Ukrainian and Arabian mares with heavy Polish stallions to produce massive, lightning fast chargers capable of carrying two hundred pounds of armor into battle at full gallop.

29
00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:27.360
[George] The iconic wooden wings of the hussars were curved forward like great raptor talons, their feathers whistling in the wind to mimic the cry of predatory eagles as the cavalry accelerated into the charge.

30
00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:39.560
[George] The tactical coordination between Sobiska's heavy cavalry and the Imperial Austrian artillery was praised by military historians as a masterpiece of combined arms maneuver in the 17th century.

31
00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:43.880
[George] Inside the besieged city of Vienna, time was running out.

32
00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:52.680
[George] For nearly two months, Kara Mustafa's army had bombarded the city with heavy artillery and dug extensive trench systems up to the city walls.

33
00:05:52.680 --> 00:06:02.440
[George] Ottoman sappers tunneled deep underground beneath the bastions, detonating enormous gunpowder mines that collapsed huge sections of the outer fortifications.

34
00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:11.480
[George] The garrison was reduced to starvation, eating cats and horseflesh, while epidemics of dysentery decimated defenders and civilians alike.

35
00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:21.960
[George] Count Starhemberg had fewer than 4,000 combatready soldiers left to defend the breaches, fighting off daily hand-to-hand assaults by ferocious Janissary shock troops.

36
00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:40.200
[George] On the night of September 11, 1683, as Ottoman miners dug their final explosives beneath the Imperial Palace walls, Starhemberg's lookouts looked up at the forested heights of the Kallenberg Mountain overlooking the city and saw signal rockets and campfires blazing against the dark sky.

37
00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:42.840
[George] The Christian relief army had arrived.

38
00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:45.400
[George] Count Starhemberg had climbed the spire of St.

39
00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:55.480
[George] Stephen's Cathedral every evening to watch the expanding Ottoman trench networks, knowing that when the Turkish sappers reached the Berg Bastion, the final assault would begin.

40
00:06:55.480 --> 00:07:08.600
[George] Ottoman artillery had fired over 100,000 cannonballs into the medieval heart of Vienna, reducing the outer bastions to jagged mounds of powdered brick and rubble where defenders fought hand to hand with clubbed muskets.

41
00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:19.840
[George] Count Starhemberg had ordered the remaining civilian volunteers and university students to fortify the inner barricades with paving stones and overturned furniture, ready to fight for every street.

42
00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:32.240
[George] At dawn on September 12, 1683, King Jan Sobiski attended Catholic Mass in a ruined chapel on the crest of the Kallenberg, dedicating the coming battle to the Virgin Mary.

43
00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:45.680
[George] As the morning mist burned away, the magnificent panorama of the Ottoman siege camps, a vast city of colorful silk tents, minarets and trenches stretching across the Danube plain, lay revealed below.

44
00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:59.120
[George] Throughout the morning and early afternoon, Austrian and German infantry under the Duke of Lorraine cleared the rough vineyards and ravines at the base of the mountains, pushing back Ottoman skirmishers and securing level ground for the cavalry.

45
00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:08.560
[George] Kara Mustafa arrogantly refused to call off the assault on Vienna's walls, trying simultaneously to breach the city and fight off the relief army.

46
00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:23.120
[George] By four o'clock in the afternoon, Sobiski had assembled the entire Christian cavalry, over 18,000 horsemen, in battle array along the ridge, the largest cavalry force ever gathered on a single battlefield in European history.

47
00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:36.320
[George] King Jan Sobiski wore an embroidered blue velvet tunic over his silvered steel breastplate, riding his favorite white charger and personally directing the deployment of 70,000 Allied soldiers along the ridge.

48
00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:48.080
[George] Lorraine's Austrian infantry cleared the fortified vineyards of Nussdorf with bayonets, driving Ottoman Janissaries from stone walls and creating the breach through which Sobiska's cavalry would deploy.

49
00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:58.640
[George] The Duke of Lorraine embraced Sobiski on the battlefield following the victory, proclaiming that the King of Poland had performed deeds of valor that surpassed the heroes of ancient antiquity.

50
00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:06.280
[George] At 5 30 in the evening, King Jan Sobiski raised his diamond encrusted saber and gave the command to charge.

51
00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:17.000
[George] Led by three thousand winged hussars with Sobiski himself riding at their head in gleaming armor, the cavalry descended the slope like an unstoppable avalanche of iron and feathers.

52
00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:21.080
[George] The earth literally shook under the thunder of seventy-two thousand hooves.

53
00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:37.800
[George] Lowering their twenty-foot lances, the hussars struck the Ottoman defensive line with catastrophic kinetic energy, shattering enemy ranks like glass, their lances impaling three or four men at once before snapping, after which the horsemen drew their broadswords and sabres.

54
00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:43.160
[George] The sheer physical shock and terrifying spectacle shattered the morale of the Ottoman army.

55
00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:50.280
[George] Janissaries and Spahis broke into wild panic, throwing down their muskets and fleeing in terror toward Hungary.

56
00:09:50.280 --> 00:10:02.680
[George] Grand Vizier Kara Mustafa fled his luxurious pavilion on a swift Arabian mare, leaving behind the sacred green banner of the Prophet, hundreds of cannons, and millions of gold ducats in loot.

57
00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:15.480
[George] The charge of the winged hussars struck Kara Mustafa's lines with the kinetic energy of an earthquake, their long hollow lances snapping like thunderbolts as they obliterated the Turkish defensive works in a single charge.

58
00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:28.680
[George] Kara Mustafa's grand vizarial standard, made of green silk and embroidered with verses from the Koran, was captured by Polish hussars inside the tent and later sent to Rome as a trophy of Christian victory.

59
00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:40.520
[George] Thousands of captured Ottoman silk banners, brass kettledrums, and jewelled daggers were brought back to Krakow and Warsaw, where they were placed in the cathedral crypts as eternal trophies of victory.

60
00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:43.000
[George] The triumph at Vienna was complete.

61
00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:47.600
[George] The two-month siege was broken in less than three hours of furious combat.

62
00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:59.600
[George] That night, King Jan Sobiski dined inside Kara Mustafa's captured silk pavilion, surrounded by heaps of Persian carpets, captured battle standards, and chests of jewels.

63
00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:05.040
[George] Sitting at a captured desk, Sobiski wrote a famous letter to Pope Innocent Xi.

64
00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:12.800
[George] Adapting Caesar's ancient phrase, Venomous, Vidimous Deus Visit, we came, we saw, God conquered.

65
00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:23.200
[George] The following morning, Sobiski entered Vienna through the battered gates, where weeping citizens knelt to kiss his boots and hailed him as the providential deliverer of Christendom.

66
00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:28.800
[George] The Battle of Vienna in 1683 was a decisive turning point in world history.

67
00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:36.080
[George] It permanently halted the expansion of the Ottoman Empire into Europe and began the long Ottoman retreat from the Balkans.

68
00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:47.440
[George] For Poland Lithuania, it was the crowning military glory of the Commonwealth, immortalizing King Jan Sobiski and the winged Hazars as the valiant knights who saved the Western world.

69
00:11:47.440 --> 00:12:01.200
[George] When Emperor Leopold returned to Vienna, he met King Jan Sobiski on horseback outside the city walls, offering a stiff, ungracious acknowledgement of the Polish monarch's providential rescue of the Austrian Habsburg throne.

70
00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:14.960
[George] King Jan Sobiska's victory at Vienna forever turned the tide of Ottoman expansion in Europe, inaugurating the Great Turkish War that liberated Hungary and established the military glory of the Polish cavalry in the annals of history.

71
00:12:14.960 --> 00:12:27.000
[George] King Jan Sobisky's historic victory at Vienna forever ended the Ottoman threat to the heart of Western Europe, securing his immortal legacy as one of the greatest warrior kings in human history.

72
00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:30.760
[George] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

73
00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:42.920
[Rebecca] Next week, our European Chronicle continues with episode 229, Poland Lithuania Part 2, the Commonwealth of Nobles, The Tragic Liberum Veto, and the Three Partitions.

74
00:12:42.920 --> 00:12:52.840
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

